      ":question/worked-solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=11$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot 11\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+22\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=5,$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+22\\pi )=5.$\n\nSubstituting $n=-1$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=5,$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-2\\pi )=5.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+22\\pi )+5-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-2\\pi ) & =\\frac{2}{5}(5)+5-\\frac{5}{3}(5) \\\\  & =2+5-\\frac{25}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
